WEBVTT

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.190
In this place so unreal

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.860
I wait for dawn to come

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.990
Farther and farther, like the sky I saw that day

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.240
What a joke this hope is

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:22.790
I acted as if I had forgotten

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.290
Farther and farther, like the dream I saw that day

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.180
I just want to know the meaning of it

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.350
Within this scattered time

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.140
Along with the breath of ours

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.100
And of this pain that never goes away

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:48.610
Even though the roaring of the sea muffles the voices

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.530
Darkness engulfs everything ahead

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.570
The light that passes through our fragile hearts

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.990
Won't go away

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.790
We only relied on our compass

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.920
We believed you

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:14.800
While we stand here

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:19.930
We're searching for the unseen future

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:29.860
Zipang

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.410
30 degrees to port on the horizon.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.280
I think it's the Tsurigizaki lighthouse.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.910
Navigator! I think I see Mount Tateyama!

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.620
We'll enter the Uraga Channel in an hour.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.920
- Full speed ahead.
- Full speed ahead!

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.880
- Full speed ahead!
- Keep an eye on the tides!

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.050
We've come home at last.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:59.090
Even if that's a beacon from 1942,

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.300
that is...

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.010
That is without a doubt, the Yokosuka Harbor.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.640
It's been 10 minutes since our last course change.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.100
Next course change at 22:45.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.480
Ashika Island Light house at 270 degrees, 2.3 miles.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.530
I repeat. The next station is at 2.26 miles...

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.530
We're right on track to enter the harbor.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:21.410
The Return

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.220
Listen carefully please.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.680
In 7 days, Takumi Kusaka will be...

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.550
...waiting for you inside Room 403 of the...

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.720
...Yokohama Hotel New Grand.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.620
He's waiting for me?

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.370
What's going on anyway?

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.670
This is it.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:28.850
Are you going to break down the door?

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.560
Or will you just knock nicely?

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.560
Are you undecided?

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.570
Lieutenant Tsuda,

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.110
so you came at last.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.450
Lieutenant Kusaka.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.120
Please come in.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.420
I'm glad you came.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.210
There's something I want to...

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:55.920
...tell you before I leave Japan.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.960
Leave Japan?

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:04.090
Oh man, this is taking us forever.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.100
I wish I could just fly there.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:07.890
There's no hurry.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.600
Japan's not going anywhere.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.890
Regardless of the year,

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:13.900
it'll always be there.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:15.560
O-O-O-Ouch!

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.900
Can't you be more gentle?

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.690
I'm being very gentle already.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.110
You're the one who told me to hurry up, didn't you?

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.700
- Off you go!
- Thank you, Lieutenant!

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.120
I also can't wait to see what the...

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.080
...Japan of 1942 actually looks like!

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.410
Alright, next!

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:35.750
What's up with you?

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.960
Um, well, I'm not sure what to do.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.800
Should I expose the Mirai to the world of 1942?

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:45.130
Or wait and write about 1942 if we ever return to our time?

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.680
Which one's more likely to earn me a Pulitzer Prize?

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:49.680
I see.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:51.770
Well, once we get ashore,

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.680
I'm going to get myself some more film!

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.320
Pilot boats spotted!

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.150
Distance 1000 yards!

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.610
That's an old tugboat.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:07.160
The fact that they did not send a military ship...

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.780
...means that the Imperial Navy...

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.200
...doesn't officially recognize that we are docking at Yokosuka.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.380
Incoming message from the pilot boat!

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:19.130
It's telling us to drop anchor...

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:23.010
...750 meters east of Sarushima Island.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.760
Off Sarushima Island?!

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:30.430
Executive Officer

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:29.010
I think we should follow their orders for now.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.600
XO, come with me.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.350
Sir.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.140
I see.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.850
It's all been planned.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:39.230
If we anchor there,

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:42.110
the island will hide the ship from anyone on land.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.030
But Captain, in 1942,

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:46.200
there should be artillery all over...

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.450
...Sarushima Island, right?

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.700
We'll be surrounded by Japanese guns, huh?

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:55.500
We'll be in a position with a high risk of direct attack.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.460
Captain, I think we need some protection.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:02.960
Very well, let's send a message to the Yokosuka Headquarters.

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:06.090
We are 2500 yards away from the jetty.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.050
Prepare to dock!

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.230
What?!

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:19.900
In order to ensure our safety,

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.520
we would like to negotiate the conditions of our stay here.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.400
Please send someone for a meeting...

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.150
...on this ship tomorrow morning at 0800 hours.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.570
If no one is sent,

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.700
then we will take it to mean that this ship...

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.660
has been given permission to enter the Uraga Channel,

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:35.370
and the whole of Tokyo Bay.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.210
To, Yokosuka Commander-in-Chief Noboru Hirata,

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:42.880
From, Mirai's Captain, Saburo Umezu.

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:46.670
Looks like they are covering themselves well.

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:48.970
Someone with authority...

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:50.970
Just who are they talking about?

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:54.100
Shall we send a spy? Or someone to sneak on board or something?

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.520
If it goes well, we might be able to...

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:58.270
Who are we going to send?

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:00.190
We'd just be sending a hostage!

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:03.150
Remember, this matter is supposed to be unofficial!

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:06.860
If we're not careful, the Army might get wind of the ship!

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:08.860
Admiral Yamamoto may have made the decision,

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.740
but this isn't Truk! It's Tokyo Bay, for goodness sake.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.080
Maybe we can cause an "accident" to sink the ship?

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:17.410
In the harbor, we have a couple Destroyers,

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:20.080
as well as the Yokosuka Air Squadron at our disposal.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.920
It's going to take 10 years for you guys to reach a decision!

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.920
Your honor! Why are you here?!

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:38.060
I'll go.

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:40.850
Please wait, your honor! It's too dangerous!

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.150
We have no idea who those people are!

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:45.730
I have a pretty good idea.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.320
They are Japanese as well.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.240
And even if something was to happen,

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.820
the loss of one elderly man isn't going to...

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.240
...really hurt the war effort, right?

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:02.540
Captain, incoming message from Yokosuka Headquarters.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:06.880
This is?!

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.390
I don't need to have an escort.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:26.230
It's been a while since I piloted a ship.

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:27.480
Your honor!

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:29.360
I've been a ship's captain...

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.490
...longer than all of you.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:42.580
Contact the Destroyers.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:44.290
Have them surround that ship immediately.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.040
Yes sir!

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.470
I'm surprised.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.760
To think it's Mitsumasa Yonai himself.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:57.680
He was the Prime Minister, and the Navy Minister.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.260
Right now, he's in the reserves, and a Navy Admiral.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:02.730
He's very powerful in the Navy.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.600
Unless it's absolutely crucial, he wouldn't get involved.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.520
To think a man like him is coming aboard alone...

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:15.240
I think it implies that they want to keep this under wraps.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:32.550
So this is... the messenger from the 21st Century!

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:53.400
I'm Yonai.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:56.150
Mirai's Captain, Saburo Umezu.

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:58.910
I commend you getting here safely.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:01.830
Yes. Thank you very much.

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:05.370
I see your ship took some damage from the looks of it.

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:09.790
Yes. We were attacked by the Americans off Saipan.

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:12.750
It was an attack from a carrier and 40 dive and torpedo bombers.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:16.760
So how many men did you lose?

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.510
We lost 5 men, with a dozen or so injured.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.430
In that case,

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:24.270
I'd like to pay my respects to these men.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.310
Sir.

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:29.560
Bathroom Number 2

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:34.780
A burial at sea is appropriate,

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:37.900
but if possible, a land burial at Yokosuka would be...

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:39.780
I see.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:42.830
In that case, let me make the arrangements for them to have...

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:44.830
...a proper burial at the Bamonzan cemetery then.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.660
That is very kind of you.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:52.790
Hey Yanagi,

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:55.880
I heard he was the head of a Sumo School. Is that true?

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:57.670
A tall and big man,

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.680
with slit eyes like an actor,

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:01.590
and an Iwate Accent!

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:04.430
He helped disarm the navy after the surrender.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.350
Admiral Yonai. Yes, that's him.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:10.100
No getting around it! My heart is pounding like crazy!

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:12.860
Someone as important as him has come on board alone?

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:13.860
Why?

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:15.480
Admiral Yonai is like...

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.690
...an elder brother to Admiral Yamamoto.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:19.400
They're the real leaders of the Navy.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:22.700
Both Commanders have now come on board to welcome the Mirai.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.660
I certainly hope so.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:31.290
Private Yagio!

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.170
What's the matter, Yanagi?

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:37.000
I'm begging you! Let me bring the coffee into the...

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.460
...Captain's Room instead!

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:40.260
- But...
- It'll be fine!

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:42.800
I've gotten permission from the XO and the officer on deck!

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:44.800
Please!!

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:46.810
So?

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:51.020
Is that old man really more amazing than Admiral Yamamoto?

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:52.730
Why, of course!

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:54.770
Even though his results were below average when...

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:56.320
...he graduated from the Naval Academy,

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.570
he eventually became the Commander of Yokosuka base,

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:00.900
then Commander-in-Chief of the combined fleet,

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:02.490
then Navy Minister,

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:05.160
then finally he became the Prime Minister of Japan.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:07.240
He's in retirement right now,

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:09.080
but after the collapse of the Tojo Faction,

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:12.080
he will once again be reinstated under Prime Minister Suzuki,

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:14.080
and use all of his energy to end this war.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.670
He doesn't look that impressive to me though.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:20.550
Well, then, go ahead.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.880
Yes! See you later then.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.680
Duty officer entering!

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:33.560
Repairs, resupply,

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:35.520
and shore leave for the crew.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:39.440
That is all we're asking for.

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:42.440
The reason why we've requested you on board,

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.410
is to guarantee our crew's safety.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:47.910
We need safety assurances from the Navy.

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:51.870
To us, this Japan is just like a foreign country.

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:56.500
If we are attacked for any reason,

00:12:56.500 --> 00:13:00.050
we will defend ourselves to the best of our abilities.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.510
However, we are not going to engage in meaningless battles.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.100
So what is the Imperial Navy's position then?

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:12.890
I understand.

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.690
I will remember that.

00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:16.150
So,

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:19.520
what do you plan to do with this ship afterwards?

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:20.480
Sir?

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:22.320
In 10 years time...

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.240
No, what about in 50 years...

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:29.410
You all have a few more years left than this old man.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:32.750
Our main aim is...

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:36.080
...to go back to the 21st Century.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.830
But right now we have no clue how to do that.

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:41.000
Until the day we can go back,

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.170
we have to continue on with our lives...

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:45.470
...as a Japanese from the 21st Century.

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:50.140
You've probably heard about my discussion...

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:52.890
...with Admiral Yamamoto in Truk.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.850
Not long from now, the battle in the Solomon Islands...

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:57.350
...at Guadalcanal will result in...

00:13:57.350 --> 00:13:59.690
...massive causalities if those men aren't rescued.

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:01.150
If we can save those men,

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:04.440
and save countless lives in the process,

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.200
then we will assist in a rescue operation...

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:08.860
...in whatever way we can as fellow Japanese.

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:12.740
Even if that means changing the course of history as we know it.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:18.000
The Japanese today are still bent on Imperial conquest.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:19.960
It's still a hundred years too early.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:22.590
This is what I think.

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:26.260
Yamamoto and...

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:28.470
...that young Officer Takumi Kusaka.

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:31.390
Together with Ishiwara from the Army.

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:33.350
They're definitely up to something.

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:35.930
I commanded a ship too,

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:38.810
and I understand your love for it.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:40.650
I think...

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:43.570
...the best way forward would be for this ship...

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:45.570
...to be blown up and scuttled in Yoksuka harbor!

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:17.560
6 Destroyers sighted! Coming in fast!

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:19.560
Distance 2000 yards.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:22.730
Doesn't sound like a welcoming party!

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:27.190
All the ships in the dock have been mobilized?!

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:29.490
I think they mean to surround us.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:33.370
More ships are approaching from Kisarazu and Yokohama!

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.700
Looks like they've recognized our ship as a military vessel.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:40.540
We can now get a good look at how they react to an emergency.

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:43.670
Is that so? That's okay.

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:45.840
There's no need for us to take any action.

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:50.470
There's no need for worry.

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:53.390
That's all only for show, boys with toys.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:55.180
They're not going to attack.

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:02.060
After the war, the citizens of Japan...

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:05.650
...have clearly chosen the right path and the right leaders.

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:09.650
This much I can tell after meeting with you.

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.150
This much is enough.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:13.490
Mr. Yonai.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:17.740
The ship's damage was caused by an attack from American planes,

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:19.540
but the one who started the battle...

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:21.540
...is a man named Taki with the Navy Headquarters.

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:25.750
He told me that there are others who also want to...

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:27.710
...destroy this ship.

00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:33.680
This war must not be won to ensure the survival of Japan.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:35.680
No matter how many sacrifices we have to make,

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:38.260
the militant faction of the Army must not succeed.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:39.970
Mr. Yonai,

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:43.520
are you saying that Japan needs to lose this war?

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:46.860
All throughout history, there have been countless cases of...

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:49.820
...countries that collapse after victory in war.

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:51.820
On the other hand, many countries that are defeated,

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:54.700
end up prospering in the years following.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.910
After meeting with you people,

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:00.290
I have realized the latter is true.

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:03.540
Right now, Japan is...

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:05.790
...on the edge of a precipice.

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:11.960
Through its present course of Imperial conquest,

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:16.220
the military is driving the country towards the edge.

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:19.430
If they don't wake up soon, destruction will follow.

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:23.480
And I don't think that they will wake up,

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:27.900
unless they're hit with the reality of defeat.

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.650
Unfortunately, the Japanese are...

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.240
...not strong enough to admit that their...

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:34.860
...present course of action is a mistake,

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:38.030
or to pull out of the pact with the other Axis powers.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.160
And that is why,

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:42.160
the militant army faction has been...

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:45.160
...manipulating things to continue fighting.

00:17:45.790 --> 00:17:48.460
The sacrifices made during this war...

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:51.550
...will affect the Japanese people for 100 years.

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:59.430
I'm not too familiar with this...

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.430
...Lieutenant Taki you mentioned,

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:06.730
but I think he's afraid of the power you possess with this ship,

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:09.770
and he and others have decided to take matters...

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:12.770
...into their own hands in order to put a stop to you.

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:15.650
I apologize on behalf of the Navy!

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:26.040
Thank you for the excellent coffee.

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:30.290
I'll make arrangements for your repairs,

00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:32.670
re-supply, and the shore leave for your crew.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:34.960
I will radio you details later.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.920
I thank you in advance.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:39.890
Captain Umezu, a word of caution.

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:43.600
please do not forget that you and your crew are...

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:45.520
...strangers in this era.

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:47.020
Mr. Yonai,

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:49.480
a total of 3 million Japanese people,

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:52.730
both soldiers and civilians, will be lost in this war.

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:56.570
You are telling me to overlook this fact.

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:58.070
But I am someone...

00:18:58.070 --> 00:18:59.990
...who can't turn a blind eye to the loss of even...

00:18:59.990 --> 00:19:01.820
...one person's life if it can be prevented!

00:19:01.990 --> 00:19:04.620
A defeated country can be revived again.

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:09.000
But a life that's lost cannot come back again!

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.460
I can't just stand by and let those people die!

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:18.300
Mr. Kadomatsu,

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:21.180
you must make decisions based on the...

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:23.010
...future survival and prosperity of Japan!

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:29.180
If this war is going to end in three years,

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:32.650
I would like for you to wait quietly for it to end!

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:35.650
This is for the best.

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:38.190
The knowledge and power that you possess,

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:39.900
should instead be...

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:41.610
...put to use in the post war revival!

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:53.880
We did not ask to become mere bystanders in the war.

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:55.540
We are...

00:19:55.540 --> 00:19:58.380
...members of the SDF, we have to try to save lives!

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:03.550
Yet he is asking us to sit back and watch 3 million people die,

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:05.930
even when we know we can help them?

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:23.070
Zipang.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:26.160
So all the while you've been thinking about this crazy idea?

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:30.250
Lieutenant, can I believe what you told me?

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:32.120
That your plan is to...

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:36.080
...avoid a crushing defeat by securing an early peace treaty,

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:37.880
and rescue the Japanese people from further hardship?!

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:41.050
If everything went as planned,

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:44.130
the Mirai should have docked at Yokosuka by now.

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:47.510
Just her presence alone should have a...

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:50.640
...large impact on the Imperial leadership.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:52.930
These next six months,

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:55.270
will be our last chance at an early peace treaty.

00:20:58.020 --> 00:20:59.610
I understand now.

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:02.780
I will believe you, and what you have said.

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:06.030
Other than that, I need to clarify one more thing.

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:09.280
You said you were leaving the country.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:11.290
Where will you go?

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:14.620
I shall go to Manchuria.

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:16.670
Manchuria?!

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:19.500
It's the first step...

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:26.930
...to Zipang.

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:38.230
Right now, I need to report to the Military High Command.

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:41.530
But... I still find it hard to believe.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.900
Messengers from the future?

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:46.700
Those who do believe it are out of their minds.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:53.830
Yokosuka Central

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:53.830
for Tsuihama
and Kanezawa

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:17.470
Passing through the distant time

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:22.670
Just peacefully

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:34.480
Without losing your way, you walked towards me

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:47.360
How many times have I grasped tightly those small hands of yours?

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:54.000
Not being able to do anything

00:22:54.570 --> 00:23:00.070
I kept on praying

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:11.890
Sorrow or agony isn't enough

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:27.570
That's why now as if I wanted to make up... I'm looking at you

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:43.100
At the end of the day, we are Japanese...

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:44.720
And this is definitely Japan.

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:46.680
I need to see my father and clarify some things.

00:23:46.810 --> 00:23:48.690
To find out what our connection is to this world.

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:50.310
What exactly is going on?!

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:52.690
History has been altered, hasn't it?!

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:54.690
The Mirai can be erased from this world.

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:57.320
This is the final decision we have made.

00:23:57.320 --> 00:24:00.110
Yes, everything started with Kusaka and me.

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:03.740
And everything won't be resolved until I confront and stop him.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:05.240
Next Episode: The Place of Return

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:10.210
This story is purely fiction. The names of the characters and organizations that appear have no connections with actual people and organizations.
